ogical differences within mobile payments, proposing a comprehensive definition that emphasizes the transfer of funds using mobile devices. HASSA's work aids in the standardization and advancement of mobile banking and payment systems, contributing to a clearer understanding of the processes involved in mobile financial transactions [Data: Entities (80); Relationships (83, 84)].\n\n## Collaborative research efforts in mobile payments\n\nThe community is characterized by collaborative research efforts among its members. HARASIM and GRIMA, for instance, have collaborated on studies about consumer payment preferences, highlighting the interconnected nature of the community. Such collaborations enhance the depth and breadth of research in mobile payments, contributing to a comprehensive understanding of consumer behavior and technological advancements in the field [Data: Relationships (780)].\n\n## Borcuch's contributions to mobile payment technologies\n\nBorcuch is an author who has been referenced in discussions related to mobile payment technologies. They have provided a definition of mobile payments, describing them as transactions conducted using mobile devices, with a focus on the convenience and accessibility offered by these technologies. Borcuch's work underscores the growing importance of mobile technology in modern financial systems, contributing to the broader understanding of digital payments [Data: Entities (81); Relationships (100, 85, 86)].\n\n## Zarańska and Zborowski's collaborative work\n\nZarańska and Zborowski have collaborated on applying a definition of mobile payments as payments made using mobile devices. Their joint efforts reflect the collaborative spirit within the community, contributing to the standardization and clarity of mobile payment terminologies. This collaboration is indicative of the community's focus on enhancing the understanding and development of mobile payment systems [Data: Entities (82, 83); Relationships (87)].", "# Poland'