omposed Theory of Planned Behavior and the Technology Acceptance Model, underscoring its foundational role in behavioral science [Data: Relationships (172, 164)].\n\n## Technology Acceptance Model's application in mobile banking\n\nThe Technology Acceptance Model (TAM) is a widely recognized framework for explaining and predicting the acceptance and use of information technology services. It focuses on perceived usefulness and ease of use as key determinants of technology adoption. TAM has been extensively applied in mobile banking studies, providing insights into user acceptance and continued use of these technologies [Data: Entities (142); Relationships (162, 165)]. Its application in mobile banking underscores its relevance in understanding consumer behavior in the digital age.\n\n## Decomposed Theory of Planned Behavior's detailed framework\n\nThe Decomposed Theory of Planned Behavior (DTPB) offers a more detailed understanding of the factors influencing human behavior by breaking down the components of the original TPB. It is particularly useful in studying consumer behavior in mobile banking, providing insights into the specific beliefs and influences driving consumer decisions [Data: Entities (141); Relationships (169, 172, 171, 173)]. The DTPB's detailed approach allows for a comprehensive analysis of psychological and social factors impacting consumer behavior.\n\n## Influence of key theorists: Ajzen, Taylor, and Todd\n\nAjzen, Taylor, and Todd are prominent theorists within this community, each contributing significantly to the development and application of behavioral and technology acceptance models. Ajzen is renowned for developing the Theory of Planned Behavior, while Taylor and Todd co-proposed the Decomposed Theory of Planned Behavior [Data: Entities (127, 128, 129); Relationships (169, 158)]. Their work has been instrumental in applying these theories to various fields, including mobile banking, highlighting their impact on understanding consumer behavior and technology adoption.\n\n## Unified Theory of Acceptance and Usage of Technology's role\n\nThe Unified Theory of Acceptance and Usage of Technology (UTAUT) is another model used to study consumer acceptance of mobile banking. It explains user intentions to use technology and subsequent usage behavior, complementing the insights provided by the Technology Acceptance Model and the Theory of Planned Behavior [Data: Entities (143); Relationships (165, 163)]. UTAUT's role in this community underscores the importance of understanding user intentions and behavior in technology adoption.", "# Polasik and Maciejewski on Mobile Payments\n\nThis community is centered around the collaborative work of Polasik and Maciejewski, who have significantly contributed to the understanding of mobile payments and banking technologies in Poland. Their research highlights the complexities of mobile payments, particularly through the integration of communication technologies like GSM and NFC. The community's structure is defined by the relationships between these researchers and the technologies they study, providing valuable insights for stakeholders in the financial sector.\n\n## Polasik's contributions to internet and mobile banking\n\nPolasik has made substantial contributions to the understanding of internet and mobile banking, particularly in the context of Poland. His research provides a comprehensive view of the evolving banking technologies, focusing on both technical and economic dimensions. Polasik's work is instrumental for financial institutions and developers as they navigate the challenges of implementing and maintaining mobile banking services. His insights into the costs associated with these technologies are crucial for stakeholders looking to leverage them effectively [Data: Entities (77)].\n\n## Collaboration between Polasik and Maciejewski\n\nThe collaboration between Polasik and Maciejewski is a key aspect of this community. Together, they have emphasized the 